WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.020
SIERRA: So, the Why It Matters team, like&nbsp;
everyone else, is home, and it’s hard, things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:15.000
are scary.I don’t know about you, but I have&nbsp;
been having endless video chats with my friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.640
and family. And, aside from drinking wine while&nbsp;
showing off completed puzzles or homemade bread,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:27.480
we’re mostly talking about two topics. Health and&nbsp;
the economy. We bounce back and forth. What starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:32.880
as a question about how someone is feeling turns&nbsp;
into an update about who lost their job. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.700
conversations are happening everywhere - nobody&nbsp;
wants to get sick, nobody wants to lose their job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:44.520
and it can feel like it’s one or the other. As&nbsp;
people deal with these worries, some politicians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:49.800
and business leaders have called for a return to&nbsp;
work in order to save the economy - even at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:57.060
expense of lives.It just keeps coming up, and&nbsp;
so we decided, hey, let’s pick up the phone and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:02.580
ask two of our fellows to help us understand&nbsp;
the problem. One an expert on health, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:07.440
other an expert on the economy. Just a heads up&nbsp;
that this episode is going to have a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:13.020
style than our other shows. It’s essentially two&nbsp;
conversations without most of our usual music and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:19.080
narration and pizzaz. Don’t worry though, we’ll&nbsp;
be back to our regular format, and non-coronavirus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:24.720
programming, for our season two premiere in&nbsp;
two weeks. But for now, I’m Gabrielle Sierra,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:31.560
and this is Why It Matters. Today, saving the&nbsp;
economy and saving lives...do we have to choose?

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:41.280
SIERRA: Ok, first up Tom Bollyky. Senior&nbsp;
fellow for Global Health at the Council.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.660
He’s getting a lot of these calls, so&nbsp;
hopefully he’s not tired of answering.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.820
Tom Bollyky, how are you?

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:58.680
BOLLYKY: I’m okay, you know, grappling&nbsp;
with the shutdown like everyone else.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:04.100
SIERRA: Yeah, what is your work from home&nbsp;
situation? She asks while sitting in her closet.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:12.840
BOLLYKY: So, he answered while sitting in my&nbsp;
bedroom. Well, we have three small kids, so you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:18.360
know, that's a challenge. And we live in the city&nbsp;
of D.C, so there is no great backyard for them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:24.860
to run around in. So they bounce around the house&nbsp;
more or less like excited molecules at this point.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:31.080
SIERRA: So we have officially made it to April.&nbsp;
Felt like we would never get here. Longest March&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:36.360
on record, I believe is the expert opinion. Do&nbsp;
you think April's going to be worse than March?

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:41.220
BOLLYKY: April in the United States will&nbsp;
definitely be worse than March. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:49.500
because by and large, it takes about three&nbsp;
weeks for people to start showing clinical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:54.780
symptoms. So starting to get physically&nbsp;
ill from having contracted the virus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:58.980
it spreads, as everyone knows&nbsp;
at this point, exponentially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:05.100
with a lot of the cases occurring among&nbsp;
people who have unreported infections.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:09.900
SIERRA: So I want to ask you about something&nbsp;
that I keep seeing people arguing about on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:14.520
social media, even though I'm trying to avoid&nbsp;
some of this stuff pretty, pretty hard. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:20.820
do you feel like we have to choose between&nbsp;
saving the economy and saving lives?

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:26.040
BOLLYKY: No, I think saving lives or more&nbsp;
specifically keeping people from getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:32.460
infected is really the only way to save the&nbsp;
economy. The reality of this is, is that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:39.060
are not going practically to be able to go back to&nbsp;
work until we get this back under control. So the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:48.360
best economic policy we have is health policy at&nbsp;
this moment. We can have a bad economic impact of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:55.020
this pandemic with good policies, we’ll still end&nbsp;
up there, or we could have a much worse economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.020 --> 00:04:00.660
impact of these with bad health policies, plus&nbsp;
all the lives lost. And, I choose the former.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:05.340
SIERRA: But I've heard people say that if&nbsp;
we don't spend money saving the economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:11.640
many more lives could be lost in the long run&nbsp;
due to recession. So do recessions kill people?

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:16.500
BOLLYKY: They do. Recessions do kill&nbsp;
people. There have been a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:22.440
studies that looked at the great recession.&nbsp;
We saw health impacts, particularly for men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:31.680
and racial and ethnic minorities, and it ranged&nbsp;
from a lot of our diseases of despair that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:38.400
exists out there already, in terms of suicide or&nbsp;
alcohol or substance abuse, traffic fatalities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:44.220
safe harm, declining fertility, you name&nbsp;
it, you see that in recessions. And we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:50.580
just saw it a dozen years ago. So we're going&nbsp;
to see that again. But again, the option here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:57.600
is not preventing a recession by allowing&nbsp;
this virus free reign to run rampant. It's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:03.180
do we want this to be a shorter recession or do&nbsp;
we want this to be a deeper and longer recession?

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:08.040
SIERRA: So the idea is to focus sort&nbsp;
of on the immediate and the short term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:11.160
and that will help, naturally, the long term.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:15.900
BOLLYKY: Absolutely. You know, China is&nbsp;
starting to head back to work. South Korea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:21.180
which had its first case the same day&nbsp;
the United States had its first case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:28.500
is back to work. You know, we can do this. We got&nbsp;
off to an awfully slow start. But it can be done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:33.000
and the sooner we can do it, the better for us,&nbsp;
both from an economic and a health perspective.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:39.660
SIERRA: So I've read that half the national&nbsp;
stockpile of ventilators has already been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:45.600
sent out, which sounds really scary. So if&nbsp;
we want to stimulate the economy and fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:50.760
the pandemic at the same time, I don't really&nbsp;
understand why we can't put more people to work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:54.740
making masks, the protective gear,&nbsp;
the ventilators, the things we need.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:59.220
BOLLYKY: So we can, but you know,&nbsp;
we need to mandate this. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:03.060
going to be still supply chain issues&nbsp;
about can we make all the components,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:07.740
can we get all that? But you know, we can&nbsp;
manage that. What is difficult to manage is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:13.740
to be doing this in April instead of doing&nbsp;
it in January, February, even early March.

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:19.500
SIERRA: So what choices has the&nbsp;
US made in situations like this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:23.820
in the past? Are there any past&nbsp;
success stories we can emulate?

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:32.520
BOLLYKY: Yeah, so in the past, in World War II and&nbsp;
in the Korean War, we granted the executive branch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:40.860
broad authority to regulate industry so that&nbsp;
we could ramp up supplies that are needed for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:46.260
the nation’s defense, we are in that kind&nbsp;
of moment. Now the president is talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:52.620
about doing that this week on a limited basis&nbsp;
and that’s great and I'm glad for it and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:57.780
should. But we are awfully late for this wave.
But here's one thing I do want to emphasize,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:04.560
unfortunately, is that even when we get this under&nbsp;
control, this isn't going away. This is just the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:09.780
first wave of this. Now if we can manage to get&nbsp;
ahead of it, we can do what some other nations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:15.240
have done, which is to have good surveillance&nbsp;
and testing and really minimize the effect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:22.080
future waves. But until there is a vaccine, or&nbsp;
a really effective therapeutic set can reduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:26.760
people from getting sick from this, we're&nbsp;
going to keep seeing waves. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:31.560
there are more than a million cases globally.&nbsp;
It's a virus that isn't going to go away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:38.580
There's no sign that it's going to go away with&nbsp;
weather, so ramping up production is good for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:43.620
those second waves. I just wished we had done&nbsp;
it earlier, so it was good for this wave too.

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:48.300
SIERRA: So has the timeline on&nbsp;
a vaccine become any clearer?

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:53.880
BOLLYKY: So, it's a year and a year and a&nbsp;
half away. That's what we're looking at.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.880 --> 00:08:00.000
The other thing to know about the vaccine is&nbsp;
once we have it, we still have to make it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:06.720
the kind of volumes that can address people's&nbsp;
needs. You know, how do we do that? But even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:11.220
once the vaccine becomes available, it'll&nbsp;
take a little time for everyone to get it.

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:15.000
SIERRA: So I shouldn't be pinning&nbsp;
all my hopes to that vaccine, huh?

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:16.980
BOLLYKY: Not in the short term. In the long term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:24.360
I'm a believer in human innovation. What I do&nbsp;
think you need for that to have its effect is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:28.920
a realistic and honest assessment&nbsp;
of the challenges we face and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:36.120
urgency in addressing them. But when you have&nbsp;
that, I, you know, fundamentally believe in us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:42.600
to have that innovation to address that. We just&nbsp;
need to mobilize it sooner than we have been.

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:46.020
SIERRA: What does the end game of&nbsp;
this pandemic look like to you?

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:52.140
BOLLYKY: The medium term, and people should&nbsp;
be honest about this, so I'm gonna try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:57.660
be in this podcast, is that you know, even in&nbsp;
the countries that have done well with this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:01.920
they're still seeing cases. They get&nbsp;
cases from people returning from abroad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:07.740
with infections like sparks, you know, you&nbsp;
need to control before they set off a wildfire.&nbsp;

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:12.420
That's going to be the reality for&nbsp;
a year or two years with this virus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:19.500
that we will have that risk. So this is not going&nbsp;
to immediately go back to the way it was. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:27.060
the medium term of this. The long term is there&nbsp;
are only really two, either we develop a vaccine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:32.820
or at least 60% of the population needs&nbsp;
to become immune. The challenge with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:39.600
latter route is it will come at a great human&nbsp;
cost. So I would like to bet on the former.

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:46.500
SIERRA: I guess this is a hard question to answer,&nbsp;
but do you see any silver linings in all this?

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:53.340
BOLLYKY: Let me put it this way. Pandemics&nbsp;
put a mirror to the societies they inflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:59.340
and what they expose about ourselves&nbsp;
in terms of our vulnerabilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:05.340
in terms of the limits of our health systems,&nbsp;
in terms of the dysfunction of our politics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:11.880
in the past those have spurred reforms, whether&nbsp;
it's the building of our public health systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:18.060
or the construction of our great sanitation&nbsp;
networks in cities. All these came as a result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:27.300
of past pandemics of cholera, or tuberculosis and&nbsp;
the like. We will have those opportunities here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:33.780
We will not be better for having experienced&nbsp;
this pandemic. It could affect as many as 40&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:38.400
to 70% of the world's population. We will not&nbsp;
be better off for having had that experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:46.260
but we will have an opportunity at the end to&nbsp;
at least learn from it, and I hope we take it.

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:52.560
SIERRA: Thank you so much. I hope you and&nbsp;
your family are, you know, good, and don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:58.500
drive each other crazy, but take these moments&nbsp;
to be together, and yeah, that you stay safe.

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:00.720
BOLLYKY: Thanks. You too. Take care.

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:07.980
SIERRA: So, according to Tom, we don’t&nbsp;
have to choose between saving lives and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:12.420
saving the economy. For the time being&nbsp;
they’re the same thing - the best way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:18.120
help the economy is to stay home and prevent&nbsp;
infections. And that’s a hard pill to swallow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:23.040
What’s good for the economy as a whole&nbsp;
has real costs - people lose their jobs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:28.620
businesses fail. So, to understand the best&nbsp;
way to contend with this double-edged sword,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:32.880
we decided it was time to call someone who&nbsp;
has spent his life thinking about the economy.

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:41.820
SIERRA: Sebastian Mallaby, hello, how are you?

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:49.560
MALLABY: Hey, Gabby, I'm great. I'm in London.&nbsp;
I’m in what my wife calls a cross between a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:56.280
multigenerational Victorian family because&nbsp;
we've got three generations. My dad, us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:02.640
two teenagers. It's a cross between a Victorian&nbsp;
multigenerational family and a We-Work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:06.240
because we're all working away at different&nbsp;
desks, at different parts of the house.

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:12.300
SIERRA: It definitely sounds like a full house.&nbsp;
So for the people at home, which I guess is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:17.100
everyone right now, including you and me. Can&nbsp;
you just tell me what you do for the Council.

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:22.500
MALLABY: I work at the Council on Foreign&nbsp;
Relations as a senior fellow focusing on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:25.860
international economics. And&nbsp;
so I follow what's going on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:30.600
in the global economy and I've written&nbsp;
some books on the history of finance.

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:36.240
SIERRA: So I am in New York City and&nbsp;
I would be remiss if I didn't ask you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:39.980
about the UK. How is it feeling&nbsp;
there? What's going on right now?

00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:45.720
MALLABY: Well, we're in lockdown just like&nbsp;
in New York. So everybody's supposed to stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:52.560
inside their house from nearly all the time. You&nbsp;
are allowed out to take your one daily outing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:57.900
to do some exercise for up to an hour.&nbsp;
You can go for a walk or a bike ride. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:03.300
go on walks because the weather's been&nbsp;
pretty great. And London can often be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:08.160
crowded and polluted, but right now it's&nbsp;
not polluted and it's not crowded. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:12.620
you can have a very nice kind of&nbsp;
exploration of this historic city.

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:19.320
SIERRA: So, here in the US there's&nbsp;
been this push to go back to work and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:24.060
you know, despite the virus and to fight&nbsp;
through and save the economy. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:29.720
do you think that we have to choose between&nbsp;
saving the economy and fighting the virus?

00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:37.560
MALLABY: I think that for now we don't have to&nbsp;
choose because whether you are thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:45.660
the economy or thinking about public health.&nbsp;
You would choose to lock down and to try to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:50.700
this pandemic under control. I think, Gabby,&nbsp;
the tough decision is going to be, not now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:59.160
but in two or three months from now when the&nbsp;
virus has moved on, you know, some people will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:04.980
have gotten it, and therefore, so long as they've&nbsp;
survived, they're going to be immune.And the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:13.500
people who are immune, the less it's obvious&nbsp;
that it's worth telling everybody to isolate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:19.440
You see what I mean? So I think as time goes by,&nbsp;
we will reach a point where it'll be fair to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.340 --> 00:14:25.560
people should go back to work, even though you&nbsp;
haven't eliminated all of the public health risk.

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:30.480
SIERRA: I know here in the US I've been reading&nbsp;
that the government is going to be sending money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:36.360
to individuals and to small businesses. So how&nbsp;
does this actually work? What's the goal there?

00:14:36.860 --> 00:14:44.340
MALLABY: The goal is to try to keep&nbsp;
everybody afloat so that when the virus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:50.520
finally plays itself out, and maybe we get&nbsp;
a vaccine, you know, a year or so from now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.620 --> 00:14:59.520
people will be able to go back and resume their&nbsp;
lives with as little damage as possible. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:07.140
don't want people to incur enormous debts so&nbsp;
that when the virus has gone they're still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:12.000
desperately financially underwater. You&nbsp;
know, this is an enormous financial hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:17.520
it will be the worst recession, the&nbsp;
deepest recession since the 1930s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:24.720
it will be much worse than 2008, 2009, in terms&nbsp;
of the reduction in economic activity, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:32.340
you know, we're in a medically induced economic&nbsp;
coma, you know, most people are being told, do not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:38.820
work, do not produce stuff, do not be economically&nbsp;
active. And when that happens, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:48.000
economic activity will collapse. So, you know, the&nbsp;
economy is going to shrink by 20 or 30% month on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:54.060
month in the second quarter of this year. And&nbsp;
that means that to get through that quarter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.060 --> 00:16:01.500
when people are not earning money, you need to&nbsp;
spend savings. Many Americans don't have savings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:08.040
so then they would have enormous hardship. And,&nbsp;
if the ones who do have savings spend all those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:13.620
savings, they're going to be in a weak state&nbsp;
afterwards. So it's better for the government to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:20.640
give them money and for the extra debt that you're&nbsp;
creating because of this massive recession, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:25.620
that should be public debt rather than private&nbsp;
debt, because the public sector, the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:33.060
is going to be better at managing that debt and&nbsp;
paying it back and surviving despite the debt.

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:37.260
SIERRA: But I mean, is a one time&nbsp;
payment enough to accomplish those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:40.920
goals? That seems like a lot with one installment.

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:45.720
MALLABY: No, you're right. I mean, I think it's&nbsp;
gotta be more than that. And I believe that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:50.280
you know, come May or thereabouts, you&nbsp;
know, I wouldn't be surprised to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:56.600
Congress pass the supplemental stimulus&nbsp;
to give additional help to people.

00:16:56.600 --> 00:17:02.340
SIERRA: I was in New York during&nbsp;
9/11 and I remember the big push was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.340 --> 00:17:05.820
you know, spend money, support the&nbsp;
economy, support your local places.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:10.940
Is that sort of the ideal situation&nbsp;
with your stimulus check now too?

00:17:10.940 --> 00:17:17.460
MALLABY: The classic line was, you know, come&nbsp;
to New York, watch a movie, go out to dinner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:23.100
stay in a hotel. Now you've stimulated the New&nbsp;
York economy and you've paid it back for 9/11.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.100 --> 00:17:28.500
Now you can't, right, because the movie, the, you&nbsp;
know, Broadway shows are closed, the restaurants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.500 --> 00:17:35.640
are closed, and the hotels pretty much aren't&nbsp;
operating either. So we have a simultaneous hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:41.880
both to demand, in other words, spending is&nbsp;
going down, but also certain types of supply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:48.780
aren’t there either because businesses are&nbsp;
closing down, they can't operate. So I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:53.880
the money this time is less about go off and spend&nbsp;
it. It's more about take this and try to survive.

00:17:53.880 --> 00:17:59.340
SIERRA: So a lot of people are losing their&nbsp;
jobs. I know I have several friends and many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.340 --> 00:18:04.920
more that are going to face job loss and&nbsp;
everyone's filing for unemployment. So is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:09.780
the US going to run out of money to pay all&nbsp;
these people for their unemployment benefits?

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:15.060
MALLABY: You know, that's a great question,&nbsp;
Gabby. It gets into a very, very interesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.660 --> 00:18:22.140
experiment that we're going to be running in the&nbsp;
next few months. If you think back to 2008, one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:31.740
the amazing policy surprises was that the federal&nbsp;
reserve could create unlimited amounts of money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:38.460
and use it to bail out the financial system.&nbsp;
And there was no apparent penalty for that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.060 --> 00:18:44.700
In the past, you would have said, hey, if the&nbsp;
Central Bank just prints money, you're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.700 --> 00:18:51.480
get inflation. This time there was no inflation&nbsp;
and without inflation, there was no reason that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:57.600
the Central Bank should stop printing more money,&nbsp;
and it carried on doing so-called quantitative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.600 --> 00:19:02.820
easing where it basically creates money and pumps&nbsp;
it into the economy. It went on doing that for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:10.860
quite some time after the 2008 crisis. So suddenly&nbsp;
we had this realization that Central Bank power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.520 --> 00:19:18.780
was way bigger than we had suspected before.&nbsp;
Now this time we're running the next experiment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.780 --> 00:19:25.920
which is about government budget power, the&nbsp;
Treasury's power to have payments to American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:31.320
families and American businesses&nbsp;
almost without limit. Can that be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:34.740
done in the way that Central Bank money&nbsp;
printing was done almost without limit?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:41.880
Now, there are a lot of economists who think yes&nbsp;
right now because inflation is nowhere in sight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:50.880
therefore, the Central Bank can create money and&nbsp;
it can use that money to buy government bonds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.880 --> 00:19:56.880
And when the Treasury sells government bonds to&nbsp;
the Central Bank, the Treasury gets lots of cash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.880 --> 00:20:04.320
and it can give that cash to Americans freely. And&nbsp;
this process could continue really to an enormous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:08.940
extent. And there will not be inflation, there&nbsp;
will not be a crash in the dollar, there will not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:13.860
be any adverse consequence. And so you have this&nbsp;
sort of free license to basically create money.

00:20:13.860 --> 00:20:16.020
SIERRA: Well, what's the potential downside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.020 --> 00:20:18.960
to that? That kind of sounds a&nbsp;
little bit too good to be true.

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:25.080
MALLABY: It does sound too good to be&nbsp;
true. The downside is that government debt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.080 --> 00:20:30.960
will increase. But the mitigating&nbsp;
thing is that the government debt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:37.020
will be owed to the Federal Reserve, which would&nbsp;
have bought the debt, and the federal reserve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:41.760
is ultimately part of the government. So the&nbsp;
government would be kind of owing money to itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.660 --> 00:20:48.900
and so that isn't actually terribly damaging.What&nbsp;
happens is the government has a bunch of debt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.900 --> 00:20:53.220
The Treasury has to pay interest on that debt.&nbsp;
It pays the interest to the owner of the debt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.220 --> 00:20:56.820
the owner of the debt is the Central Bank,&nbsp;
the Central Bank collects all that interest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.820 --> 00:21:01.920
and under the rules of the Central Bank,&nbsp;
it pays back its profits, guess where,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:07.620
to the Treasury. So the Treasury basically pays&nbsp;
the money to the fed and gets it back from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.620 --> 00:21:14.520
fed, and it's a wash. So it is very much like&nbsp;
alchemy. You are printing money out of nothing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:21.120
It's creating gold by magic. So it's kind of&nbsp;
weird, but it's one of those things where,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:25.860
you know, if you think about it carefully, you&nbsp;
can't think of a reason why it wouldn't work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.460 --> 00:21:31.140
so long as people are okay with it. Of&nbsp;
course, if everybody decided to panic all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.140 --> 00:21:37.080
at once and run away from the dollar and buy&nbsp;
gold or Bitcoin, then the dollar would crash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.080 --> 00:21:42.360
and we would all be in trouble. But I don't think&nbsp;
that's going to happen. So I think we may discover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:43.020 --> 00:21:48.120
that you can increase government debt&nbsp;
without it actually crippling the economy.

00:21:48.120 --> 00:21:52.860
SIERRA: I mean, it seems like it's worth&nbsp;
a try, at least in a situation this tough.

00:21:52.860 --> 00:21:57.300
MALLABY: Right. Because we have&nbsp;
certainty about the cost of not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.300 --> 00:21:59.760
helping people out, that they&nbsp;
will be real human hardship.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:07.260
And we have uncertainty about the economic&nbsp;
consequences of racking up a lot of extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.260 --> 00:22:13.020
government debt. And so I agree with you, it's&nbsp;
an experiment that we should be willing to try.

00:22:13.020 --> 00:22:19.680
SIERRA: Nice, so I can take at least one of my&nbsp;
immediate worries off my long list. Not worry as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:25.920
much about that specific thing and just focus on&nbsp;
everything else that is continuously worrying me.

00:22:25.920 --> 00:22:28.440
MALLABY: I'm glad I've given you a silver lining.

00:22:28.440 --> 00:22:33.660
SIERRA: Thank you for that relief. So, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.660 --> 00:22:39.420
I am an eternal optimist even in the face&nbsp;
of, you know, what's going on right now. Are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:39.420 --> 00:22:42.740
there any other silver linings that&nbsp;
you sort of see coming out of this.

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:52.800
MALLABY: So on Saturday, my sister had a birthday&nbsp;
and she lives about a half hour walk from my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.800 --> 00:23:01.020
house, so I cooked up a plan where I went on my&nbsp;
walk, my other sister came on a walk too. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.020 --> 00:23:10.020
we brought our kids along and a big banner that&nbsp;
said happy birthday in huge letters and some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.020 --> 00:23:16.200
gifts, and we put the gifts on the doorstep, and&nbsp;
then we retreated a safe distance and called my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:24.000
sister on cellphone. And when she opened the door,&nbsp;
what she could see was the gift down on the steps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:30.540
in front of her house and the big banner, and we&nbsp;
were all safely distant and, you know, medically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:30.540 --> 00:23:35.580
obedient to the public health rules. But we did&nbsp;
manage to have a family birthday celebration.

00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:41.940
SIERRA: Oh, I love that. It was my&nbsp;
father's 65th birthday on Friday. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:46.020
we sent food delivered and I had put like&nbsp;
a little note and the local restaurant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.020 --> 00:23:51.360
they sent him this big card and it was&nbsp;
signed by all of them, and you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:55.800
it was just really lovely. So I guess we're doing&nbsp;
the best we can with these birthdays, right?

00:23:55.800 --> 00:23:57.000
SEBASTIAN: I like your story.

00:23:57.000 --> 00:24:00.060
SIERRA: Sebastian, thank you so, so, so much.

00:24:02.480 --> 00:24:09.060
SIERRA: So, do we have to choose between saving&nbsp;
the economy and saving lives? Whether you look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.060 --> 00:24:14.220
at it from the economic angle or the health&nbsp;
angle, right now the answer is a clear no.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:14.220 --> 00:24:20.040
According to our experts, the best thing for the&nbsp;
economy is still to stay home, prevent further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:26.400
infections, and keep as many people alive as&nbsp;
possible.But there will eventually be a time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:26.400 --> 00:24:31.380
when the United States, and other countries, have&nbsp;
to make tough choices about when healthy people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.380 --> 00:24:37.140
should return to work. Even as additional waves&nbsp;
of the virus threaten to appear.In the meantime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:37.140 --> 00:24:51.420
stay safe, be well, and we’ll see you soon.&nbsp;
